            <ArticleTitle>Association of the degree of adiposity and duration of obesity with measures of cardiac structure and function: the CARDIA study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2434-40</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/oby.20865</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Examine whether there are independent influences of a greater degree of adiposity and longer duration of obesity on cardiac structure and function.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Participants of CARDIA were 18-30 years when they underwent a baseline examination in 1985-86. Seven follow-up examinations were conducted every 2-5 years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Among 2,547 participants who underwent an echocardiogram at the year 25 examination and were not obese at baseline, 34.4 and 35.5% were overall (BMI ≥ 30 kg m(-2) ) and abdominally obese (waist circumference: men: &gt;102 cm; women: &gt;88 cm) at year 25, respectively. A greater degree of overall and abdominal adiposity at year 25 were each associated with a greater left ventricular (LV) mass (P &lt; 0.001), LV volume (P &lt; 0.001), LV mass-to-volume ratio (P &lt; 0.001), left atrial dimension (P &lt; 0.001), and ejection fraction (P &lt; 0.05) after adjustment for duration of obesity and other risk factors. In contrast, a longer duration of overall obesity was associated with a greater LV mass (P = 0.003) and a trend for a lower ejection fraction (P = 0.07).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A greater degree of adiposity is strongly associated with concentric LV remodeling in midlife, while the cumulative effects of a longer duration of overall obesity during young adulthood contribute to concentric remodeling predominantly by increasing LV mass.</AbstractText>
